t's the smart play here. It would be a waste of a potentially game-winning play.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to pass.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: My hand is disjointed, containing a Bomb (3s), pairs (2, 6, 7), and several loose singles (5, 8, J, Q, A) with no straights. While the Bomb (♠3 ♥3 ♣3 ♦3) is a valid move to beat the opponent\'s Full House (JJJ55), doing so would deplete my strongest asset early in the game. My remaining hand would be difficult to clear due to the lack of sequences and the presence of low singles. Additionally, with both Jokers and two 2s still unaccounted for, it is highly likely an opponent could beat my next lead, leaving me trapped with weak cards and no bomb.\n\nReason: It is strategically better to Pass. Using the Bomb now exposes me to counter-attacks from Jokers or higher bombs and leaves me with a weak, disconnected hand that cannot easily maintain control. Preserving the bomb and my high cards (2s, A) allows me to wait for a better opportunity to regain initiative or punish a weaker play later in the game.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
